in @prism
in @meta
in @nl
in @glass
in @io
in @code

# @mirror/realisation — the per-Rust-file discriminator.
#
# The recognition (2026-06-08, Alex → Mara, T21): the substrate has
# been operating an implicit @io-vs-substrate-realisable discriminator
# since `docs/specs/kintsugi-self-hosting.md` was written (2026-06-04
# horizon). That spec's per-file "Status:" line ("stays in Rust" /
# "can be grammar NOW" / "needs @io boundary") IS a discriminator
# verdict — every Rust file in `bootstrap/src/` gets one. The
# discriminator has been running in MARA'S HEAD for weeks; the 26-29
# substrate-pull recognitions logged in the rug pull doc
# (§5, 2026-06-08) are the training set the discriminator learned
# from. T21's second half declares the discriminator in substrate
# vocabulary so T22 (`mirror kintsugi <file.rs>`) can run it
# automatically against the bootstrap, without Mara doing it by hand.
#
# === Discipline ===
#
# A `@mirror/realisation` instance IS:
#
#   - a classifier on Rust source: given a parsed Rust AST (via
#     @code/rust), the discriminator emits a typed verdict naming
#     which side of the glass wall the file lives on (`@io` boundary
#     vs `@mirror/*` substrate-realisable) and — when the verdict is
#     substrate-realisable — WHICH mirror altitude already declares
#     what the file does (the `target` field).
#   - a lens INTO the substrate-pull recognition surface: each
#     classification reads `kintsugi-self-hosting.md`'s typed re-
#     declaration (the @code/rust altitude grammar, the @mirror/loss
#     family, the @mirror/data family, etc.) and asks: does the
#     substrate already declare this Rust file's content at one of
#     those altitudes? If YES → substrate-realisable; the file is a
#     CANDIDATE for kintsugi-on-Rust migration to `.mirror` source.
#     If NO and the file calls a non-mirror surface (syscall,
#     subprocess, vendor SDK, libgit2) → @io; the file STAYS at the
#     bootstrap altitude. The discriminator's verdict IS the lens
#     reading.
#   - composable under the transparency monoid: per the lens family
#     discipline (`shards/mirror/lens.mirror`), realisation verdicts
#     compose under `transparency<p>` per
#     `shards/mirror/loss/transparency.mirror`. A file that is 80%
#     substrate-realisable (most actions have mirror-altitude
#     declarations; one function calls an opaque libgit2 routine)
#     emits `partial(opacity_map)` with the libgit2-calling lines
#     located as opacities — pareto-front material for the audition
#     altitude (`@mirror/spectral/audition`), not auto-apply.
#
# === The 30th-instance substrate-pull recognition ===
#
# Per `[[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]`, with the rug
# pull doc's tally (29 instances logged through 2026-06-08):
#
# The "@io vs substrate-realisable" partition has been operating
# under three names already:
#
#   1. `docs/specs/kintsugi-self-hosting.md` §1 — "@io boundary?"
#      column on every Cargo dependency; "Status:" line on every
#      Rust file (1180 lines of prose discrimination).
#   2. `AGENTS.md` § "The Glass Wall" — "@io is the substrate's only
#      legitimate non-mirror surface" (the binary partition stated
#      as discipline).
#   3. `boot/std/epistemologic/property/glass_wall.mirror` — the
#      `glass_wall(g)` property that REFUSES to compile a grammar
#      claiming to be substrate-realisable while reaching into a
#      non-@io surface.
#
# Three prior names; one primitive. The 30th instance: this shard
# names the discriminator that the three names had been implicitly
# evaluating. The recognition rate is accelerating (5 instances in
# the second 36-hour window per the rug pull doc §1); T21 is the
# tick whose substrate-pull check turned up a THREE-NAMED ancestor
# at the recognition altitude.
#
# === The carrier shape: realisable_file ===
#
# The discriminator's output is a typed record per Rust file. Four
# fields, derived from the three-state classification in
# `kintsugi-self-hosting.md` plus the location field needed for
# `@io/cargo`-style boundary localization:
#
#   path        @code/rust file the discriminator was run against.
#   verdict     three-state classification carrier (Pass / Partial /
#               Failure per the substrate's `transparency` floor):
#                  Pass(@io)             → boundary file; STAYS in Rust
#                  Pass(@mirror/<X>)     → substrate-realisable;
#                                          CANDIDATE for kintsugi-on-
#                                          Rust migration; target
#                                          altitude is named
#                  Partial(opacity_map)  → mixed; some functions are
#                                          substrate-realisable, others
#                                          are @io; per-function refine-
#                                          ment is a later tick
#   target      the mirror altitude that subsumes this file when the
#               verdict is substrate-realisable (e.g. `@mirror/loss`
#               for `loss.rs`, `@code/mirror` for the parser-related
#               files, `@mirror/spectral/oscillate` for
#               `oscillate.rs`). `none` when the verdict is `@io`.
#   altitude    structural shorthand: `@io` for boundary; `@mirror`
#               for substrate-realisable; absent for partial pending
#               per-function refinement.
#
# This shape is intentionally per-file. Per-function refinement (some
# functions in a `.rs` file are `@io`, others are `@mirror/<X>`) is a
# later tick — T22 consumes the per-file primitive; per-function
# Partial cases land an opacity_map that names the @io-reaching
# functions as located opacities, and a later refinement tick teaches
# the discriminator to walk the function-level AST.
#
# === Three actions: classify / is_substrate_realisable / target_altitude ===
#
# The discriminator surface is intentionally narrow. Three actions:
#
#   classify(f) -> realisable_file
#     The structural primitive. Takes a parsed Rust AST; emits the
#     full realisable_file record. Body discharges by lifting the
#     `kintsugi-self-hosting.md` prose classification into a typed
#     AST-pattern match against `@code/rust`'s declared altitudes.
#     What T22 calls per file.
#
#   is_substrate_realisable(f) -> verdict
#     The binary projection of classify. Returns Pass when the file
#     is substrate-realisable at SOME altitude; Failure when the
#     file is @io; Partial when the file mixes both. The verdict
#     surface other consumers (mosaic's settle_on; the audition
#     altitude; CI gates) read without needing the full
#     realisable_file record.
#
#   target_altitude(f) -> ref
#     The targeting projection. Returns the @mirror altitude that
#     subsumes the file when classify's verdict is substrate-
#     realisable. Returns `@io` when the verdict is @io. Body
#     reads the family roster (@mirror/lens, @mirror/loss,
#     @mirror/data, @mirror/spectral, @code/mirror, @io) and picks
#     the one whose grammar already declares what the file does.
#     T22 consumes this directly: given the target altitude, T22
#     produces the first fracture proposal by diffing the file's
#     AST against the target altitude's grammar — the fracture IS
#     the substrate-pull gradient at the file altitude.
#
# === The training set: 29 substrate-pull recognitions ===
#
# Per the rug pull doc §5, the discriminator's training set is the
# 29 instances of `[[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]`
# logged through 2026-06-08. Each instance is a labeled (Rust-code-
# shape, substrate-altitude) pair the substrate has already
# evaluated to "substrate-realisable; target altitude = X":
#
#   #16 lift (foundational, not zoom)               -> @prism (the five operations)
#   #18 eigensheaf (spectral decomposition)         -> @epistemologic/math/sheaf_laplacian
#   #19 five operations are Hodge primitives        -> @prism
#   #20 eigenspace (composition foundation)         -> @mirror/spectral/composition (future)
#   #21 prophecy (derive fractures from topology)   -> @mirror/fracture/predicate (future)
#   #22 score family root                            -> @mirror/spectral/score
#   #23 glass IS lens (four names, one primitive)    -> @mirror/lens + @mirror/loss
#   #24 oscillate_witness (next-oscillation)         -> @mirror/spectral/oscillate
#   #25 morphism_context (per-candidate slice)       -> @mirror/spectral/morphism
#   #26 shift(oid, T) typed-capability primitive     -> @mirror/spectral/portal
#   #27 template render (one operation, many uses)   -> @code/mirror
#   #28 "path was already half-built" (json lift)    -> @mirror/data
#   #29 "presence IS the signal" (SCM_RIGHTS)        -> @mirror/spectral/portal
#
# Each row is one classify() training example. The discriminator's
# body (when it lands, T22 or later) reads the @code/rust AST and
# walks the family roster: does this file's structural shape match
# `@mirror/lens`'s family? `@mirror/loss`'s? `@code/mirror`'s? The
# 29 prior recognitions teach the discriminator what "shape-match"
# means at each altitude.
#
# === Why this family lives at @mirror/realisation ===
#
# Pattern-matching against existing `@mirror/*` family roots:
#
#   @mirror/lens       observes the substrate's running STATE
#   @mirror/loss       measures the substrate's running RESIDUAL
#   @mirror/data       carries the substrate's running CONTENT
#   @mirror/spec       declares the substrate's running MANIFOLD
#   @mirror/spectral   coordinates the substrate's running AGENCY
#   @mirror/store      stores the substrate's running ARTIFACTS
#
# The realisation family observes the substrate's running BOUNDARY-
# WITH-RUST — what's already substrate-realisable, what's not yet.
# It sits at the mirror altitude because realisation is a mirror-
# altitude concern (asking "is this file mirror-shaped?" is a mirror
# question; asking it of a Rust file is a mirror-about-Rust
# question). The naming `realisation` over alternatives:
#
#   - vs `@mirror/realization` (US spelling): the substrate uses
#     British spelling for substrate vocabulary (`realisation`,
#     `colour`, `behaviour`) per the rug pull doc's prose
#     consistency. Same word; one canonical form.
#   - vs `@mirror/classify`: classifying is what the discriminator
#     DOES; realisation is what the file IS (substrate-realisable
#     or not). Naming the substrate-side noun, not the verb.
#   - vs `@mirror/code/realisation`: the discriminator operates on
#     Rust files specifically (the input type is @code/rust.ast),
#     but the OUTPUT is a mirror-altitude verdict about
#     substrate-realisability. The output altitude pulls the family
#     placement to @mirror, not @code. Same precedent as
#     @mirror/spec (operating on .spec files; landing at @mirror).
#   - vs nesting under @kintsugi or @code/rust: the discriminator
#     is consumed BY kintsugi (T22 calls realisation to produce
#     fractures); operates ON @code/rust; but neither of those is
#     its substrate home. The home is where the discriminator
#     reports — and the report is at mirror altitude (the substrate
#     side of the partition, not the boundary side, not the
#     boundary-crossing-operation side).
#
# === The carrier vs the verdict ===
#
# The realisable_file record is the FULL output (path + verdict +
# target + altitude). The verdict is the BINARY projection
# (substrate-realisable / @io / mixed). Both surfaces are useful:
#
#   - T22 (the kintsugi-on-Rust pilot) consumes the FULL record:
#     given target_altitude, it diffs the file against the altitude's
#     grammar and emits a fracture proposal.
#   - Mosaic's settle_on consumes the VERDICT: a project can declare
#     `settle_on { substrate_realisation_complete }` and mosaic
#     checks whether every Rust file in the bootstrap classifies as
#     `@io`, with no substrate-realisable holdouts. The verdict IS
#     the convergence signal at the bootstrap altitude.
#   - The audition altitude (`@mirror/spectral/audition`) consumes
#     the OPACITY MAP from Partial verdicts: every per-file mixed
#     classification surfaces as a candidate tournament input;
#     auditions resolve which functions migrate first.
#
# Three readers, three projections, one carrier.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
# The discriminator's mathematical lineage:
#
#   realisability             Kleene (1945); Realizability in
#                              intuitionistic mathematics. A
#                              proposition is realisable iff there
#                              EXISTS a constructive witness. The
#                              discriminator asks: does the substrate
#                              already CONSTRUCTIVELY DECLARE what
#                              this Rust file does? If yes → realisable;
#                              the witness IS the substrate
#                              declaration at the target altitude.
#   classifier                Aristotle (Categories, ~350 BCE);
#                              Linnaeus (Systema Naturae, 1735). The
#                              taxonomic discipline. The substrate's
#                              version: typed taxonomy of the
#                              bootstrap's surface.
#   boundary-condition        Hilbert (1900); Boundary value problems
#                              in mathematical physics. The
#                              substrate's @io family IS a boundary
#                              condition on the realisation
#                              discriminator's domain.
#
# Per `[[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]`: `prism
# @mirror/realisation { ... }` declares the family IS a prism. The
# five-operation block below is the canonical declaration form (cf.
# `shards/mirror/lens.mirror`, `shards/mirror/loss.mirror`,
# `shards/mirror/data.mirror`). Each consumer specialises from there.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/kintsugi-self-hosting.md (the prose form
#               of the discriminator; the 1180-line training set for
#               classify()),
#               roadmap/2026-06-08-rug-pull.md §6 (T21 + T22 named
#               as the kintsugi-on-Rust prerequisite + pilot; the
#               29-instance training-set tally) + §11 (the forward-
#               look: bootstrap Rust shrinks to pure-@io when the
#               discriminator has classified every file),
#               docs/insights/2026-05-26-glass-wall-and-cross-wall-
#               kintsugi.md (the structural partition the
#               discriminator's verdict carries — permanent-@io vs
#               cross-wall-kintsugi candidate),
#               roadmap/wip/butterfly-self-hosting.md §5 (C1-C4 cut
#               criteria; the discriminator's verdict surface IS
#               what C3 and C4 check against).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#                      (the discriminator IS a lift FROM the prose
#                      classification IN kintsugi-self-hosting.md TO
#                      a typed substrate surface),
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]
#                      (the partition the discriminator's verdict
#                      walks).
# Substrate recognitions: [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                      (the 30th instance: three prior names —
#                      kintsugi-self-hosting.md's "Status:" line,
#                      AGENTS.md's "The Glass Wall", and
#                      `glass_wall(g)` property — have been operating
#                      this discriminator implicitly; T21 names the
#                      primitive at the carrier altitude),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-pull]] (the family pulls
#                      a prose discipline into a typed substrate
#                      surface; the prose was already running, the
#                      typed surface lets T22 execute it
#                      mechanically).
# Forward-promise to consumers:
#   - T22 (`mirror kintsugi <file.rs>` first pass): consumes classify()
#     against `bootstrap/src/gap.rs` as the smallest Rust file the
#     substrate already declares (`@mirror/spectral/oscillate` /
#     `@epistemologic/math/curvature` cover the gap data structures).
#     The first fracture proposal IS the diff between the Rust file's
#     AST and the target altitude's grammar.
#   - Per-function refinement: a later tick teaches classify() to
#     walk the function-level AST and emit per-function verdicts
#     into the opacity_map. The Partial case becomes refinable.
#   - The audition altitude pulls is_substrate_realisable verdicts
#     into the tournament's input set; the audition resolves which
#     substrate-realisable files migrate first.
#   - Mosaic's settle_on pulls the verdict aggregated over the
#     bootstrap; the kintsugi-on-Rust exit condition is "every Rust
#     file in `bootstrap/src/` classifies as @io" (the butterfly
#     C3 / C4 cut criteria in substrate vocabulary).
# Related shards: io.mirror (T21's first half; the @io family root
#                  this shard's verdicts target when the file is a
#                  boundary file; landed in the same tick because each
#                  half is the other half's substrate prerequisite),
#                  code.mirror + code/rust.mirror (the @code/rust
#                  altitude whose `ast` type is classify's input),
#                  code/mirror.mirror (one of the family roster
#                  altitudes the discriminator targets when a Rust
#                  file's content is mirror-source-shaped — the
#                  parser-related files),
#                  mirror/loss.mirror (the family root one of the
#                  Rust files lifts to per the prose classification),
#                  mirror/lens.mirror (the family root the LSP / CLI
#                  / shell / MCP transport files lift to),
#                  mirror/data.mirror (the family root the data-
#                  format files lift to),
#                  mirror/spectral.mirror (the family root the
#                  oscillate / morphism / kintsugi-coordination
#                  files lift to),
#                  mirror/loss/transparency.mirror (the carrier the
#                  discriminator's Partial verdicts ride; the opacity
#                  map names per-function @io-reaching call sites).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/mirror/realisation.mirror` declares
# `@mirror/realisation` as the family root; sub-shards (e.g.
# `@mirror/realisation/function` for per-function refinement) live
# at `shards/mirror/realisation/<name>.mirror` when consumers pull
# them.

prism @mirror/realisation {
  focus realisation
  project realisation
  split realisation
  shift realisation
  settle realisation
}

# === altitude — the boundary partition ===
#
# Two-state carrier naming which side of the glass wall a file lives
# on. The substrate's binary partition stated as a typed alternative
# so consumers (mosaic.settle_on; the audition altitude) pattern-
# match without needing the full realisable_file record.
#
#   boundary       the file IS @io; it stays in Rust permanently
#   substrate      the file IS substrate-realisable at some @mirror/*
#                  altitude; it is a candidate for kintsugi-on-Rust
#                  migration to a .mirror declaration
type altitude =
  | boundary
  | substrate

# === realisable_file — the discriminator's full output carrier ===
#
# Four fields per `kintsugi-self-hosting.md`'s prose classification
# shape:
#
#   path      @code/rust file the discriminator ran against.
#             Substrate-pull: `ref` is the substrate's universal
#             content-reference carrier (per glass.mirror's `file:
#             ref` precedent, mirror/store.mirror's `oid = ref`,
#             code/rust.mirror's altitude-reference pattern).
#   altitude  the binary partition (`boundary` vs `substrate`).
#             When `boundary`, the file STAYS in Rust at the @io
#             altitude. When `substrate`, the file is a CANDIDATE
#             for kintsugi-on-Rust migration to a .mirror
#             declaration.
#   target    when altitude = substrate, the @mirror altitude that
#             subsumes the file (e.g. `@mirror/loss` for loss.rs,
#             `@code/mirror` for the parser files,
#             `@mirror/spectral/oscillate` for oscillate.rs). When
#             altitude = boundary, target IS the @io species
#             (e.g. `@io/cargo`, `@io/llvm`, `@io/fs`). `ref`
#             carrier because the target is always a substrate-
#             declared altitude reference.
#   verdict   the located-opacity carrier per
#             `@mirror/loss/transparency`. `Success` when the
#             classification is unambiguous; `Partial(opacity_map)`
#             when the file mixes substrate-realisable functions
#             with @io-reaching ones (the per-function refinement
#             surface); `Failure(opacity_map)` when the file resists
#             classification entirely (rare; should land as a
#             substrate-pull-realize on @mirror/realisation itself
#             — the discriminator's miss IS a substrate gap).
type realisable_file = {
  path: ref,
  altitude: altitude,
  target: ref,
  verdict: transparency,
}

# === classify — the structural primitive ===
#
# Given a parsed @code/rust AST, emit the full realisable_file
# record. The body (`\`) discharges by walking the AST against the
# family roster (@io, @code/mirror, @mirror/lens, @mirror/loss,
# @mirror/data, @mirror/spectral, @epistemologic/math/*) and
# pattern-matching the file's structural shape against each
# altitude's declared grammar. The 29 substrate-pull recognitions
# from the rug pull doc §5 are the training set the body lifts
# into a typed walk.
#
# What T22 calls per file. Per-function refinement is a later
# tick consuming this primitive — the function-level AST walk
# decomposes the file's classification into per-function verdicts
# that compose under the transparency monoid back into the file's
# verdict.
classify(f: ref) -> realisable_file { \ }

# === is_substrate_realisable — the binary projection ===
#
# The verdict-only surface other consumers (mosaic.settle_on; the
# audition altitude; CI gates) read without needing the full
# realisable_file record. Reads classify(f).verdict and returns:
#
#   Success when altitude = substrate (the file is migratable)
#   Failure when altitude = boundary  (the file stays in Rust)
#   Partial(opacity_map) when mixed   (per-function refinement
#                                      pending)
#
# Body discharges by projecting classify(f).verdict; the
# implementation is `\` because the projection IS the body —
# consumer-pull-discharge per `[[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]`.
is_substrate_realisable(f: ref) -> verdict { \ }

# === target_altitude — the targeting projection ===
#
# The @mirror altitude that subsumes the file when the verdict is
# substrate-realisable; the @io species the file targets when the
# verdict is boundary. T22 consumes this directly: given the target
# altitude, T22 produces the first fracture proposal by diffing the
# file's AST against the target altitude's declared grammar — the
# fracture IS the substrate-pull gradient at the file altitude.
#
# Body discharges by reading classify(f).target.
target_altitude(f: ref) -> ref { \ }

out @mirror/realisation
out altitude
out realisable_file
out classify
out is_substrate_realisable
out target_altitude
